ERROR REPORTS

Please send errors and suggestions to Frans Kaashoek and Robert Morris
(kaashoek,rtm@mit.edu).  The main purpose of xv6 is as a teaching
operating system for MIT's 6.1810, so we are more interested in
simplifications and clarifications than new features.

BUILDING AND RUNNING XV6

You will need Clang and LLVM with MMIX ELF support (including ld.lld,
llvm-objdump, and llvm-readobj), a host C compiler, GNU make, and
qemu-system-mmix. QEMU must support the virt machine's Linux/FDT startup
ABI, contiguous RAM, and high-MMIO devices. No external target libc is required.

With the installed tools in your shell search path, run "make qemu".
The default configuration uses two CPUs, 256 MiB of RAM, and a modern
VirtIO block device. Use "make qemu CPUS=1" for single-core operation or
"make qemu QEMUMEM=512M" to change the RAM size.
